# Ethics: Theories of Right and Wrong

> Ethics asks how we should live. Metaethics asks what moral talk
> means; normative theories build accounts of right action; applied
> ethics brings them to the clinic, the courtroom, and the farm.

## 1. The Three Levels of Ethics

@diagram tree id="philosophy-ethics-levels" layout="tree" aria="Ethics divided into metaethics, normative, and applied levels"
  Ethics -> Metaethics
  Ethics -> Normative
  Ethics -> Applied
  Normative -> Consequentialism
  Normative -> Deontology
  Normative -> "Virtue ethics"
@/diagram

| Level | Question | Example |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Metaethics | What do moral claims mean — facts, feelings, commands? | Are moral truths objective? |
| Normative | What makes actions right or wrong? | Is lying always wrong? |
| Applied | What should be done in this case? | Is euthanasia permissible? |

[definition]
**Moral absolutism** holds some acts are wrong everywhere, always.
**Moral relativism** holds rightness varies with culture or
individual. Metaethics asks which of these stances (or another) is
true — and what would make it true.
[/definition]

## 2. Consequentialism: The Greatest Good

**Consequentialism** judges actions solely by their outcomes. Its
dominant form is **utilitarianism**:

| Thinker | Contribution |
| --- | --- |
| Jeremy Bentham (1748–1832) | Pleasure and pain as the sovereign masters; the **hedonic calculus** — intensity, duration, certainty, propinquity, fecundity, purity, extent |
| John Stuart Mill (1806–1873) | **Higher pleasures** of the mind over bodily ones; "better to be Socrates dissatisfied than a fool satisfied" |

Bentham's calculus is democratic — each counts for one, nobody for
more than one. Mill answered the "swine" objection by ranking pleasures
qualitatively. **Act utilitarianism** rates each act by its own
consequences; **rule utilitarianism** rates rules by the consequences
of their general adoption.

[warning]
Objections: the trolley case suggests killing differs from letting die;
the **tyranny of the majority** could license injustice; and the theory
demands a saint's total sacrifice of every personal project.
[/warning]

## 3. Deontology: Duty First

**Kantian deontology** (from *deon*, duty) holds that rightness
depends on conforming to duty, not on outcomes. It separates:

| Imperative | Form | Example |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Hypothetical | If you want X, do Y | If you want to be trusted, keep promises |
| Categorical | Do Y, unconditionally | Keep promises — always |

The two famous formulations: act only on maxims you can will as
**universal law**; and treat humanity in every person never **merely
as a means**, but always as an end. Persons have dignity (beyond
price) because they are rational agents whose consent matters.
Objections: conflicting duties have no priority rule, and Kant's
rigorism famously forbids lying even to a murderer hunting your friend.

## 4. Virtue Ethics: Be the Right Kind of Person

Aristotle asks not "which act is right?" but "what kind of person
should I be?" — one whose character expresses the virtues, because
only virtuous activity achieves **eudaimonia** — flourishing, a whole
life going well. **Phronesis** (practical wisdom) is the master virtue
that finds the right act, since virtue lies in a **mean between two
vices**, relative to us:

| Virtue | Deficiency | Excess |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Courage | Cowardice | Rashness |
| Temperance | Insensibility | Self-indulgence |
| Generosity | Stinginess | Wastefulness |
| Truthfulness | Understatement | Boastfulness |
| Friendliness | Surliness | Flattery |

Revived in the twentieth century by Anscombe and MacIntyre, it
counters: rules and calculations cannot anticipate every case — a good
agent can.

## 5. Other Normative Theories

| Theory | Claim | Champion |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Divine command | Right is what God commands | Religious ethics; puzzles over the Euthyphro dilemma |
| Natural law | Reason reads the good off human nature | Aquinas |
| Social contract | Right is what rational contractors would agree to | Hobbes, Locke, Rousseau — and Rawls |
| Care ethics | Morality grows from relationships and responsiveness | Carol Gilligan, In a Different Voice (1982) |
| Egoism | Each ought to pursue own (enlightened) interest | Hobbes' psychology |

**Rawls' A Theory of Justice (1971)** is the modern classic: choosing
society's rules from an **original position** behind a **veil of
ignorance** — ignorant of your class, talents, or fortune — you would
pick justice as fairness: equal basic liberties and the difference
principle (inequalities only if they benefit the worst off).

## 6. Applied Ethics in One Box

[example]
Euthanasia: a utilitarian weighs intractable suffering against
autonomy; a Kantian objects that hastening death treats a person as
disposable; a virtue theorist asks what compassion with integrity
would do. Capital punishment: consequentialists debate deterrence
against the risk of executing the innocent; deontologists split over
retributive desert versus the categorical ban on killing. Animal
ethics: Bentham's question — not "can they reason?" but "can they
suffer?" — grounds Singer's equal consideration of interests (Animal
Liberation, 1975) and Regan's rights view; Kantians grant only
indirect duties via humanity.
[/example]

## 7. One Case, Three Verdicts

A runaway trolley will kill five; you can divert it to a side track
where it will kill one. Should you pull the switch?

| Theory | Verdict | Reasoning |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Utilitarianism | Pull | Five deaths averted, one caused — the arithmetic favours pulling |
| Deontology | Do not pull | Diverting uses the one as a means to save the rest |
| Virtue ethics | It depends | What would a practically wise, compassionate person do here? |

[summary]
Ethics operates on three levels — the meaning of moral talk, the
theory of right action, and its application. Consequentialism counts
outcomes; deontology demands duty and respect for persons as ends;
virtue ethics cultivates character guided by phronesis. Contract,
care, natural law, and divine command complete the map — and applied
ethics is where their differences become decisions.
[/summary]
